item.text:
Reused evidence: none.

New evidence:
- Treasury’s FY 2022 budget result shows the administration cut the deficit sharply while the economy kept adding jobs.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Joint Statement of Janet L. Yellen, Secretary of the Treasury, and Shalanda D. Young, Director of the Office of Management and Budget, on Budget Results for Fiscal Year 2022","publisher":"U.S. Department of the Treasury","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy1043","claim":"The FY 2022 deficit fell to $1.375 trillion, was half of FY 2021, $1.8 trillion lower than the deficit the President inherited, and the economy had added more than 10 million jobs with unemployment back to 3.5 percent.","locator":"paragraph beginning 'The 2022 deficit of $1.375 trillion...' and the following paragraph on jobs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Treasury/OMB statement with concrete fiscal and labor-market figures; strong for outcome context and administration attribution, though some claims are self-described.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Biden’s administration and fiscal agenda; the budget result is not purely his personal action but is directly tied to executive leadership.","contrary_evidence":["Treasury’s own framing mixes inherited recovery effects with administration choices, so attribution is partial rather than exclusive."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- BEA’s annual GDP release confirms positive growth in 2022, but also shows inflation remained elevated.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gross Domestic Product, Fourth Quarter and Year 2022 (Second Estimate)","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis","publication_date":"2023-02-23","url":"https://www.bea.gov/news/2023/gross-domestic-product-fourth-quarter-and-year-2022-second-estimate","claim":"Real GDP increased 2.1 percent in 2022, current-dollar GDP increased 9.2 percent, and the PCE price index increased 6.3 percent.","locator":"section headed 'GDP for 2022'","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary national accounts release with standard macroeconomic measures and clear target-year fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers the full 2022 year, with publication just after the target period.","ruler_attribution":"This is country-level macro context, only partly attributable to Biden because monetary policy and global shocks also mattered.","contrary_evidence":["Positive growth coexisted with high inflation, so growth alone does not establish broad prosperity."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- CPI inflation remained high through the end of 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Consumer Price Index News Release - 2022 M12 Results","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","publication_date":"2023-01-12","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/cpi_01122023.htm","claim":"The CPI-U rose 6.5 percent over the 12 months ending December 2022, while the index for all items less food and energy rose 5.7 percent and shelter rose 7.5 percent.","locator":"paragraphs around the 'The all items index increased 6.5 percent...' and 'The index for all items less food and energy rose 5.7 percent...' lines","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary statistical release from BLS; direct, authoritative inflation evidence for the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly reflects end-2022 inflation conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual rather than fully attributable to Biden, because much of inflation was driven by pandemic, supply-chain, and energy shocks.","contrary_evidence":["High inflation is a major negative for household welfare and a constraint on economic stewardship."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- Real wages fell over the year, which is the clearest household-level negative in the 2022 record.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Real Earnings News Release - 2022 M12 Results","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","publication_date":"2023-01-12","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/realer_01122023.htm","claim":"Real average hourly earnings for all employees decreased 1.7 percent from December 2021 to December 2022.","locator":"table A-1 and the opening summary of the real earnings release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary BLS release using CPI deflation and standard earnings series; strong household purchasing-power evidence.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"End-2022, directly in period.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects national real-wage conditions under Biden, but not a policy fully under presidential control because inflation and wage setting were partly outside the White House.","contrary_evidence":["Monthly nominal earnings rose, but inflation outpaced them over the year."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- The December 2022 labor market remained strong, even though that did not solve inflation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Employment Situation News Release - 2022 M13 Results","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","publication_date":"2023-01-06","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_01062023.htm","claim":"Total nonfarm payroll employment increased by 223,000 in December 2022, the unemployment rate edged down to 3.5 percent, and payroll employment rose by 4.5 million over the year.","locator":"opening summary of the December 2022 Employment Situation release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary BLS labor-market release; direct and standard source for jobs and unemployment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly at year-end 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly attributable to the broader U.S. economy and administration policies, but not uniquely to Biden alone.","contrary_evidence":["Strong employment data do not erase inflation and real-income pressure."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10"]}
- The IMF’s 2022 Article IV review gives an independent check: recovery was strong, but inflation had become broad-based.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"United States: 2022 Article IV Consultation-Press Release; Staff Report; and Statement by the Executive Director for the United States","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-07-12","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/07/12/united-states-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-staff-report-and-statement-by-the-520659","claim":"The IMF said the United States had staged a strong recovery, unemployment had returned to end-2019 levels, output was close to its pre-pandemic trend, and inflation had accelerated broadly beyond food and energy.","locator":"summary section on the IMF country report page","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral surveillance source with direct 2022 assessment; strong for macro context, weaker for direct policy attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period assessment of the 2022 economy.","ruler_attribution":"This is primarily country-level and partly attributable to Biden’s administration through fiscal policy, but the IMF explicitly treats the Fed, global shocks, and prior pandemic support as key drivers too.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF’s positive recovery language is paired with a warning that inflation was already creating hardship and needed tighter monetary policy."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- CBO estimated that the Inflation Reduction Act would reduce deficits over the decade.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Estimated Budgetary Effects of H.R. 5376, the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022","publisher":"Congressional Budget Office","publication_date":"2022-08-03","url":"https://www.cbo.gov/publication/58366","claim":"CBO estimated that enacting the Inflation Reduction Act would reduce deficits by $90 billion over 2022-2031.","locator":"Summary section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary budget scorekeeping from Congress’s nonpartisan budget office; directly relevant to fiscal stewardship.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period legislative estimate for Biden’s 2022 signature initiative.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Biden’s legislative push and signing, though the enacted bill reflects congressional bargaining.","contrary_evidence":["CBO’s estimate is about deficit effect, not a complete welfare or inflation verdict."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.10"]}
- Biden’s signing remarks on the Inflation Reduction Act are direct evidence of intent and policy design.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks on Signing Legislation To Combat Inflation, Promote Clean Energy Production, and Reduce Prescription Drug Costs","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-08-16","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-signing-legislation-combat-inflation-promote-clean-energy-production-and-reduce","claim":"Biden said the law would lower prescription-drug costs and health premiums, invest $369 billion in clean energy and energy security, create tens of thousands of good-paying jobs, and cut deficits over the next decade.","locator":"remarks from lines 69-90 and 104-115","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential signing remarks with specific policy claims; strong for intent and enactment, weaker for realized effects in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exactly in-period and tied to one of the central 2022 economic laws.","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential action and advocacy by Biden.","contrary_evidence":["The economic effects were mostly prospective in 2022 and not yet fully observable that year."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}
- The CHIPS implementation fact sheet shows a deliberate industrial-policy push toward domestic capacity and jobs.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: President Biden Signs Executive Order to Implement the CHIPS and Science Act of 2022","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-08-25","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-president-biden-signs-executive-order-implement-the-chips-and-science-act-2022","claim":"The White House said the CHIPS implementation order would lower the cost of goods, create high-paying manufacturing jobs, and make critical technologies at home.","locator":"fact sheet text around the implementation goals","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary executive-branch fact sheet; strong for policy direction and implementation architecture, weaker for realized outcomes within 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period and directly tied to Biden’s 2022 industrial policy.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden through presidential order and administration direction.","contrary_evidence":["Implementation benefits were prospective in 2022 and depended on later execution and private investment."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}
- Commerce’s CHIPS strategy adds more concrete implementation detail, including jobs and broader inclusion.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biden Administration Releases Implementation Strategy for $50 Billion CHIPS for America program","publisher":"U.S. Department of Commerce","publication_date":"2022-09-06","url":"https://www.commerce.gov/news/press-releases/2022/09/biden-administration-releases-implementation-strategy-50-billion-chips","claim":"Commerce said the CHIPS program would revitalize domestic semiconductor manufacturing, spur innovation, and create tens of thousands of good-paying manufacturing jobs and more than 100,000 construction jobs, including pathways for women, people of color, veterans, and rural workers.","locator":"implementation strategy summary and goal bullets","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary departmental implementation strategy with concrete job and inclusion targets; strong evidence of policy design and delegated execution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and immediately after the CHIPS Act became law.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Biden’s administration, though execution was delegated to Commerce.","contrary_evidence":["The strategy describes projected jobs and inclusion, not completed outcomes in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
- DOT testimony on the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law shows implementation of productivity investment and visible project rollout.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Implementation of the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act by the U.S. Department of Transportation","publisher":"U.S. Department of Transportation","publication_date":"2022-03-02","url":"https://www.transportation.gov/implementation-infrastructure-investment-and-jobs-act-us-department-transportation","claim":"Transportation Secretary Buttigieg said the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law’s bridge investment was the largest dedicated bridge investment since the Interstate Highway System and that the law would improve supply chains, daily travel, and create jobs.","locator":"March 2, 2022 Senate testimony, especially the bridge-investment and jobs paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary departmental testimony with concrete program details; credible for implementation and spending, less direct for whole-economy effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period and directly about 2022 implementation of Biden’s infrastructure agenda.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Biden’s administration through enacted law and delegated implementation.","contrary_evidence":["The testimony speaks to planned and underway projects, not complete macro outcomes in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}
- Census data show the distributional side was much weaker than the headline recovery.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States: 2022","publisher":"U.S. Census Bureau","publication_date":"2023-09-12","url":"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2023/income-poverty-health-insurance-coverage.html","claim":"Real median household income fell for White households, real median earnings fell 2.2 percent for all workers, the official poverty rate was 11.5 percent, and the SPM rose to 12.4 percent as temporary Child Tax Credit, EITC, and stimulus expansions expired.","locator":"earnings and poverty sections, especially the highlighted bullets and the paragraphs around lines 198-199 and 227-243","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Census publication with standard income, poverty, and distribution metrics; strong for household-welfare context, though retrospective.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Measures 2022 outcomes, though published in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"This is only partly attributable to Biden because the deterioration reflects the end of temporary pandemic-relief policies, inflation, and household distribution effects beyond direct presidential control.","contrary_evidence":["The report also shows some groups, including Black households, saw the official poverty rate improve, so the distributional picture is mixed."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary:
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across Treasury, BEA, BLS, Census, CBO, IMF, Commerce, DOT, IRS, and White House / APP materials. I kept the most decision-relevant, non-duplicative sources that separately covered fiscal stewardship, growth, inflation, wages, labor markets, industrial policy, and distributional outcomes.

I rejected or merged material in these buckets:
- Duplicates or near-duplicates: multiple White House op-eds, fact sheets, and signing remarks that repeated the same IRA, CHIPS, or infrastructure claims without adding a distinct factual proposition.
- Out-of-period or later retrospectives that were only useful if they reported 2022 facts already captured by official 2022/2023 releases.
- Broad institutional pages such as IRS IRA overview pages, Commerce CHIPS landing pages, and White House category pages that were useful as discovery but not as the best discrete evidence item.
- News noise and unrelated 2025-2026 pages that surfaced in search but had no relevance to Biden’s 2022 economic record.
- Weak attribution items where the link to Biden was too indirect or the claim was just general optimism without a concrete economic mechanism.
- Macro claims from government sources that were too self-described or too aspirational when a stronger statistical or budget source existed.

Remaining gaps:
- Direct evidence on 5B.2, especially Biden’s appointment and empowerment of specific economic professionals, is still thinner than the policy-and-outcome record.
- More independent evidence on whether 2022 industrial-policy actions already moved private investment, regional job creation, and household purchasing power would help tighten attribution.
- The distributional story is still only partly resolved: I have strong evidence that inflation and expiring pandemic relief hurt households, but less direct evidence on which regions and groups benefited most from Biden’s 2022 policies.
- More search would help on CEA/NEC staffing, OMB guidance, and independent evaluations of IRA/CHIPS/IIJA implementation effects in 2022 and early 2023.
